INSUFFICIENT CONTEXT. Missing: anything about passing a Depends() object as an ordinary argument to a constructor, or about getting a dependency back as a deferred callable you invoke yourself later (your caching use case).

What the sections do support: FastAPI resolves a dependency by inspecting the parameters of the callable it will call, processing them like path operation function parameters, including sub-dependencies. So Depends markers only mean something when they appear as parameters of that callable. In your code you construct greeter yourself and hand Depends(get_greeting_message) into __init__, so FastAPI never sees it and never solves it; self.greeting_provider stays a plain Depends object, which is why calling it raises TypeError. FastAPI inspects greeter.__call__, which declares no parameters at all.

The pattern the sections do document is to declare the sub-dependency on the callable FastAPI actually calls:

class GreetingGenerator:
    def __call__(self, message: Annotated[str, Depends(get_greeting_message)]):
        return {"message": message}

That resolves before your code runs. The sections describe no way to defer it.

