The response schema is generated from exactly the model you put under `model` in `responses`. FastAPI takes that Pydantic model, generates its JSON Schema, and places it in the correct place in OpenAPI (under content -> application/json -> schema, as a $ref into components.schemas). It does not add a `detail` wrapper of its own, which is why your schema shows the bare error object.

So declare a model that matches the real body:

class PlaylistNotFoundResponse(BaseModel):
    detail: HttpError_PlaylistNotFound

@app.get(
    path="/playlists/{playlist_id}",
    responses={404: {"model": PlaylistNotFoundResponse}},
)

Your raise stays as it is: FastAPI's HTTPException accepts any JSON-able data for `detail`, so the runtime body is unchanged and the documented schema now matches it. You can reuse the pattern by making one wrapper per concrete error model, or a generic wrapper parameterized on the error type.
